## 3. How the app starts (entry point)

### React SPA (`next-quest`)
There is one explicit entry file — `main.tsx` — that manually boots everything:

```tsx
// main.tsx  — YOU write this glue code
createRoot(document.getElementById("root")!).render(
  <StrictMode>
    <Provider store={store}>           {/* Redux */}
      <PersistGate loading={null} persistor={persistor}>  {/* persist */}
        <App />                        {/* router + pages */}
      </PersistGate>
    </Provider>
  </StrictMode>
);
```

### Next.js (`next-quest-nextjs`)
There is no `main.tsx`. Next.js boots the app itself.
Your `app/layout.tsx` is the root shell — called once for every request:

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx  — Next.js calls this automatically
export default function RootLayout({ children }) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>
        <StoreProvider>   {/* our custom wrapper = Provider + PersistGate */}
          <Navbar />
          <main>{children}</main>   {/* page fills in here */}
          <NotificationStack />
          <SyncEngine />
        </StoreProvider>
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

> **Key difference:** In the SPA you write the HTML shell manually. In Next.js
> the framework owns the HTML shell and you inject into it.

