## 4. How routing works

### React SPA — one big JS bundle, URL handled in JS

```tsx
// App.tsx — one file lists every route manually
<BrowserRouter>
  <Routes>
    <Route element={<Layout />}>          {/* shell */}
      <Route path="/"            element={<HomePage />} />
      <Route path="/learn"       element={<RoadmapPage />} />
      <Route path="/learn/:slug" element={<LessonPage />} />
      <Route path="/badges"      element={<BadgesPage />} />
      <Route path="/chat"        element={<ChatPage />} />
      <Route path="/settings"    element={<SettingsPage />} />
      <Route path="*"            element={<HomePage />} />
    </Route>
  </Routes>
</BrowserRouter>
```

The browser loads one `index.html` + one `bundle.js`. When you click a link,
JavaScript swaps the component — **the server is never asked again**.

### Next.js — routing IS the file system

```
src/app/
├── page.tsx              →  /
├── learn/
│   ├── page.tsx          →  /learn
│   └── [slug]/
│       └── page.tsx      →  /learn/anything-here   ← [slug] = dynamic segment
├── badges/page.tsx       →  /badges
├── chat/page.tsx         →  /chat
└── settings/page.tsx     →  /settings
```

You create a **folder with a `page.tsx`** and the route exists. No registration
needed anywhere. Dynamic segments use `[square-brackets]` in the folder name.

#### Getting the dynamic slug

| | React SPA | Next.js |
|---|---|---|
| **How to get `:slug`** | `const { slug } = useParams()` | `const { slug } = use(params)` (prop passed by framework) |
| **Where `params` comes from** | React Router hook | Next.js injects as a `Promise<{ slug }>` prop |
| **Why Promise?** | N/A | Next.js 15+ made params async to support streaming SSR |

