## 6. Redux store — the biggest structural difference

This is the most important technical change between the two projects.

### React SPA — one global singleton

```ts
// app/store.ts — module-level singleton, created once when the file is imported
import storage from "redux-persist/lib/storage";  // always localStorage

export const store = configureStore({ ... });      // ← lives forever
export const persistor = persistStore(store);
```

Then in `main.tsx`:
```tsx
<Provider store={store}>   {/* import the singleton and pass it in */}
```

This works perfectly in a browser because there's only one tab, one session,
one localStorage. The store is created once at page load and never recreated.

### Next.js — factory function + provider

```ts
// store/store.ts — a FUNCTION that creates a store on demand
export function makeStore() {         // ← returns a new store every time called
  const store = configureStore({ ... });
  const persistor = persistStore(store);
  return { store, persistor };
}
```

```tsx
// store/StoreProvider.tsx
export default function StoreProvider({ children }) {
  const storeRef = useRef(null);
  if (!storeRef.current) {
    storeRef.current = makeStore();   // ← create once per React tree, not per module
  }
  return (
    <Provider store={storeRef.current.store}>
      <PersistGate persistor={storeRef.current.persistor}>
        {children}
      </PersistGate>
    </Provider>
  );
}
```

**Why the difference?**

Next.js runs code on both the **server** and the **browser**. If you create the
store at the module level (singleton), it gets shared across every user's
request on the server — User A's data leaks into User B's session. The factory
pattern ensures each React tree gets its own isolated store.

### SSR-safe storage

```ts
// React SPA — always localStorage, always works
import storage from "redux-persist/lib/storage";

// Next.js — no localStorage on the server, so we need a fallback
function createNoopStorage() {
  return {
    getItem() { return Promise.resolve(null); },  // server: pretend storage is empty
    setItem(_key, value) { return Promise.resolve(value); },
    removeItem() { return Promise.resolve(); },
  };
}
const storage = typeof window !== "undefined"
  ? createWebStorage("local")   // browser: use real localStorage
  : createNoopStorage();         // server: use fake no-op
```

