## 8. The shell / Layout component

### React SPA — `Layout.tsx` + `<Outlet />`

`Layout.tsx` is a React Router concept. It renders the nav, then `<Outlet />`
which is a placeholder that React Router fills with the active page:

```tsx
// Layout.tsx
export const Layout = () => (
  <div>
    <header>...</header>
    <main>
      <AnimatePresence>
        <motion.div key={location.pathname}>
          <Outlet />    {/* ← active page renders HERE */}
        </motion.div>
      </AnimatePresence>
    </main>
    <NotificationStack />
    <SyncEngine />
  </div>
);
```

It gets wired in `App.tsx` as a parent route:
```tsx
<Route element={<Layout />}>   {/* parent */}
  <Route path="/" element={<HomePage />} />   {/* child → fills Outlet */}
```

### Next.js — `app/layout.tsx` + `{children}`

`app/layout.tsx` is the equivalent. Instead of `<Outlet />` it receives
`children` as a prop — Next.js automatically passes the current page in:

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx
export default function RootLayout({ children }) {
  return (
    <html><body>
      <StoreProvider>
        <Navbar />
        <main>{children}</main>   {/* ← active page renders HERE */}
        <NotificationStack />
        <SyncEngine />
      </StoreProvider>
    </body></html>
  );
}
```

> **In the SPA**, `Layout.tsx` also renders the `<html>` and `<body>` tags via
> `index.html` (handled by Vite). **In Next.js**, `app/layout.tsx` renders them
> directly in the JSX.

